From 3a10a27f63412283fff6190eb7b8e91e88414004 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: daveey <daveey@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 13 Nov 2026 21:52:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [daveey] kanban sync: claims wait for this process's first
 round at the feed head; the outbox alarm reads the last completed round

Incident (2026-11-13, autoscaled worker-a1, t_01a1d6c4e700958e; alarm card t_01a1d655b5b68523): the scaler created
canary t_01a1d64ab82d51b2 at 19:03:03, worker-a1 leased it at 19:03:10 and the run ended rate-limited at 19:03:24,
but that run end reached the Board only after 19:18:10. The Board kept the lease, the scaler logged `enrollment
failed: canary running` at 19:13 and the node was terminated. The outbox-age alarm fired at 19:15 and 19:19.

Five whys:
1. Why did the run end not reach the Board? Its run row sat in the outbox ~15 min.
2. Why? The push before a round's pulls holds every row from the first `task_runs` row on (FIFO,
   `kanban_sync_backlog.sendable_before_head`); only the push at the end of the round sends it.
3. Why was the round 15 min? A fresh host's first round has cursor 0: Resync, a full unbudgeted `/snapshot` of every
   table (measured 179 s just to read the default board's replica snapshot from worker-5), then `resync_live` and
   the full repair pass.
4. Why was there a run at all during that round? The dispatcher claims Board leases without waiting for the first
   round.
5. Why did the alarm call it a stall? `_sync_board_locked` cleared `backlog.behind` at round START, so during any
   round in progress (and before the first one completes) `kanban_sync_outbox_age._age` counted held run rows,
   while `_loop_healthy` counts a round running >= 10 min as healthy.

Fix:
- Claim gate (`hermes_cli/kanban_sync_claims.py`, checked in `kanban_db_sync.claim_gate` before the lease
  request): a process that runs, or is about to run, this host's sync loop (`kanban_sync_stop.run_loop`; the
  gateway's sync watcher marks it before it waits for the host sync lock) claims nothing on a `primary` board until
  one of its rounds on that board COMPLETED with the replica and live pulls at the feed head (`result.behind` False,
  i.e. `mark_pulled` stamped). Fails closed; one INFO line per board while held; deferral `sync_first_round`
  (origin local). Once open it stays open for the process. Shadow/off boards and processes without a sync loop
  (`hermes kanban claim` from a shell) are unchanged, except that a process without a loop honours another live
  process's loop record (`kanban/.sync-claims.json`), for a profile gateway that holds the dispatcher lock while the
  root gateway holds the sync lock. `hermes kanban sync status` prints `claims: held (first round not at head)` /
  `open`.
- Alarm truth (`kanban_sync_backlog`): `behind(path)` now reads the last COMPLETED round, and is True until a round
  of this process reaches the head. `round_started` only drops the rerun-at-once flag (`due`), so only a completed
  round that ended behind reruns at once, as before. The outbox alarm therefore ages only sendable ops while the
  host is behind or before its first round completes, the documented rule.
- The FIFO run-row hold itself (#1958, t_01a1d41c2612ebc0) is untouched.

Proof:
- New tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_sync_claim_gate.py, 9 tests, GREEN at this head. RED on main (claims module and
  test copied onto origin/main, without the gate and backlog changes): 7 fail, among them
  - a fake hub whose first round raises Resync and serves a 2-row-per-page snapshot: a dispatcher tick on the
    second snapshot page leased the card (`('running', 1, True) == ('ready', 0, False)`);
  - before any round completes, `_age` counted a 20-min-old held run row (1200 s);
  - during a round after one that ended behind, `_age` counted it (1200 s).
- Existing sync suites (79 files, 1166 tests: test_kanban_sync_*.py, the claim/dispatch claim tests, worker node,
  runs_on dispatch, gateway singleton lease) pass. test_kanban_sync_rejections.py hits the 300 s per-file cap on this
  worker both here and on origin/main (pre-existing; CI runs it).
- Live state checked: the hub's first round after a gateway restart takes ~35 s (21:14:04 lock, 21:14:39 default
  primary round), so the gate holds hub claims for about that long after each restart.
